{"acceptance_criteria":"","actor":"","agent_state":"","assignee":"Joey Thometz","await_id":"","await_type":"","close_reason":"Replaced async websockets with sync websocket-client in _notify_openclaw(). Needs on-device validation.","closed_at":"2026-03-17T22:33:18Z","closed_by_session":"","compacted_at":null,"compacted_at_commit":null,"compaction_level":0,"content_hash":"1f0edd43d9cacc671937092d785405e20bd3ee04cc0eb02c8cbd75e4834c14f2","created_at":"2026-03-17T22:26:20Z","created_by":"Joey Thometz","crystallizes":0,"defer_until":null,"description":"OpenClaw rejects chat.inject with 'invalid handshake: first request must be connect'. The _notify_openclaw() function sends connect then chat.inject, but connect response is not checked — likely an error (wrong scope, wrong token format, or connect response requires an ack). Fix: (1) log the connect response, (2) check for error in connect response before sending chat.inject. OpenClaw may require operator.admin scope — verify the token 439368c7ef3a54d50317db8d985c5b2829ab2e494ec24e26 has that scope. Also check: asyncio.run() in a daemon thread may not work cleanly — try using websocket-client (sync) instead of websockets (async).","design":"","due_at":null,"ephemeral":0,"estimated_minutes":null,"event_kind":"","external_ref":null,"hook_bead":"","id":"hackathon-dly.5","is_template":0,"issue_type":"bug","last_activity":null,"metadata":"{}","mol_type":"","no_history":0,"notes":"","original_size":null,"owner":"joey@neuralwatt.com","payload":"","pinned":0,"priority":1,"quality_score":null,"rig":"","role_bead":"","role_type":"","sender":"","source_repo":"","source_system":"","spec_id":"","status":"closed","target":"","timeout_ns":0,"title":"Fix chat.inject: connect handshake fails silently, chat.inject rejected","updated_at":"2026-03-17T22:33:18Z","waiters":"","wisp_type":"","work_type":""}
{"acceptance_criteria":"","actor":"","agent_state":"","assignee":null,"await_id":"","await_type":"","close_reason":"","closed_at":null,"closed_by_session":"","compacted_at":null,"compacted_at_commit":null,"compaction_level":0,"content_hash":"01c89a1c7f75288582467103b0daec433a6e374af11fcd6dfc27267ad79aa7ef","created_at":"2026-03-17T22:27:26Z","created_by":"Joey Thometz","crystallizes":0,"defer_until":null,"description":"Instead of switching models on dirty grid, cap GPU SM frequency via nvidia-smi. Instant effect, no model downtime. Design doc already mentions this as stretch goal. Demo story: clean grid = full freq (3003 MHz), dirty grid = capped freq (e.g. 1000 MHz). Energy receipt shows lower J/response and different tok/J. Carbon router sets freq cap instead of (or in addition to) model switch. Commands: sudo nvidia-smi -lgc 300,1000 (cap max to 1000 MHz), sudo nvidia-smi -rgc (reset). Validated on GB10 per gb10-validated.md.","design":"","due_at":null,"ephemeral":0,"estimated_minutes":null,"event_kind":"","external_ref":null,"hook_bead":"","id":"hackathon-foc","is_template":0,"issue_type":"epic","last_activity":null,"metadata":"{}","mol_type":"","no_history":0,"notes":"","original_size":null,"owner":"joey@neuralwatt.com","payload":"","pinned":0,"priority":1,"quality_score":null,"rig":"","role_bead":"","role_type":"","sender":"","source_repo":"","source_system":"","spec_id":"","status":"open","target":"","timeout_ns":0,"title":"GPU freq cap as carbon response (alternative to model switch)","updated_at":"2026-03-17T22:27:26Z","waiters":"","wisp_type":"","work_type":""}
